// @run
// @log false
// The parameter stays spelled `q0` (not `_q0`) so the file is byte-faithful to the
// rep-fuzz shape it pins; the warning that costs is declared rather than dodged.
// @warning Unused parameter `q0`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `v` is inferred as
// GRADUATED from `error-narrow-reflist-arm-of-closure-result-union.vl`, which pinned the
// EMIT reject ``is` names a declared union member with no interned arm representation`.
// This is the rep-fuzz shape `p2r ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64` (seed 99,
// case_00243): a closure-arm value union whose RESULT is itself a union of a ref-list and
// a struct, with the narrowing reading the ref-list arm out of the called result.
//
// WHY THE REJECT WENT. It was never about this composition being unrepresentable; it was
// about the arm having no interned rep, and the arm had none because
// `internFuncTypeShapesTy` had no composite-union arm — a closure RESULT that is a value
// union never interned its arms' inline shapes, so the literal's shape got `#anonN` and the
// arm's `{f: boolean}[]` spelling matched no reflist slot. With that arm added,
// `refArmUnionRetName`'s `hasObjSibling` decline (which existed only to dodge the
// downstream `array value does not match any array member of the union`) could be lifted,
// the lambda's `fnRet` pins to the declared union, and the whole shape lowers.
// `closure-arm-narrow-tag-agreement.vl` carries the full mechanism and the before/after
// tag values.
//
// `false` IS THE CORRECT ANSWER and the old header already said so: `t1` is the ref-list
// arm, so the inner `is` takes the TRUE branch and `t1[0].f` is the `false` the literal
// holds. The failure this file used to guard against was `OTHER` — the inner narrow taking
// the else branch — and that is what a regression here would print.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE STILL PINS. It is the corpus side of the rep-fuzz baseline's last line,
// whose stated GRADUATION CRITERION was "the program above prints `true`" (the fuzzer's
// spelling seeds `{f: true}`; this one seeds `{f: false}`). That line is now removed from
// `scripts/rep-fuzz-baseline.txt`, so this file is what would catch the shape regressing —
// including the specific regression the baseline was written for: enabling the shape
// interner's element dedup (unifying the refinement's two vocabularies through
// `shapeFieldElemName`) used to turn this program into a MISMATCH. A REJECT can no longer
// hide that here, because there is no REJECT left to hide behind.
//
// THE THREE REPRESENTABLE NEIGHBOURS that kept the old floor narrow all still run and are
// pinned by `refarm-union-obj-sibling-result-representable.vl`: a NAMED element type, a
// NAMED producer function, and the same union bound DIRECTLY.
function makeIt(): ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64 {
  return (q0) => ([{ f: false }])
}
function go() {
  const v: ((i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32}) | f64 = makeIt()
  const t0 = v
  if t0 is (i32) => {f: boolean}[] | {w: i32} {
    const t1 = t0(1)
    if t1 is {f: boolean}[] { print(t1[0].f) } else { print("OTHER") }
  } else {
    print("OTHER")
  }
}
go()
